description:
crate-jmap-server/src/helpers.rs:228 — now_utc_string_checked returns Option<UTCDate>, with three distinct failure modes collapsed into None:

  /// Returns `None` when:
  /// - `SystemTime::now().duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).as_secs()`
  ///   exceeds `i64::MAX` (only reachable on a corrupted clock —
  ///   approx ±292 billion years from epoch).
  /// - The negation of a pre-epoch duration overflows `i64`
  ///   (unreachable on a `try_from`-validated input but checked
  ///   defensively).
  /// - `civil_from_days` reports a year outside `i32`
  ///   (bd:JMAP-jfia.2 — between the i32-year boundary and the
  ///   i64::MAX-secs cap).

The Option shape is correct in spirit (replaces the sentinel String from .30) but erases the discriminator. A caller that wants to react differently to each failure mode — e.g. an audit logger that reports 'clock skewed pre-1970' vs 'clock skewed past year 5.7M' for different alerting paths — cannot distinguish from Option<UTCDate>::None.

The .30 finding correctly migrated *away* from sentinel String to typed-Option. But the same reasoning that argued Option > sentinel argues Result<UTCDate, ClockError> > Option<UTCDate>: structured-failure-information > 'something went wrong'.

The cost of the bigger change is symmetric:

  pub enum ClockError {
      SecsExceedI64,
      NegationOverflow,
      YearOutsideI32,
  }
  // #[non_exhaustive], with Display + Error impls.

  pub fn now_utc_string_checked() -> Result<UTCDate, ClockError>;

Migration cost: every caller of now_utc_string_checked.is_none() pattern flips to .is_err(); every caller using `?` becomes `.map_err(/* some adapter */)?`. The bd:JMAP-jfia.30 commit just landed and there's no third-party caller yet, so the cost is borne entirely inside this crate.

Workspace argument FOR keeping Option: the failure modes are all 'corrupted clock' — one logical bucket, multiple physical mechanisms. The doc-comment already lists the three modes. An audit logger wanting per-mode telemetry can grep the helper's internals to know what fired. This is a weak argument because it requires the caller to read implementation, not the contract.

Workspace argument AGAINST keeping Option: the kit is the foundation; every *-server extension will compose this helper. Audit logging is the second-most-common consumer for now_utc_string. Erasing the discriminator at the foundation forces every consumer to duplicate the read-the-internals reasoning.

The api-contract concern is in the 'underspecified' bucket: Option<UTCDate> documents WHAT can fail but the API itself doesn't carry the discriminator. A Result<UTCDate, ClockError> is the additive shape — adding variants to a #[non_exhaustive] enum at a later date is non-breaking.

Filed P2. Borderline opinion-vs-engineering: the failure modes are rare enough that operationally erasing them is acceptable, but the pattern of 'one typed error variant per failure mode' is the workspace's own preferred shape (see SetError, BackendChangesError, BackendSetError, MergePatchError — all use typed variants with non_exhaustive). This helper breaks that pattern.

If the verdict is that Option is correct, this should be added to the docstring as an explicit decision record: 'three failure modes are intentionally collapsed because they all signal "abandon timestamping" rather than "react conditionally".'
